Arad","H5738":"Eder","H4317":"Michael","H3472":"Ishpah","H3109":"Joha","H4918":"Meshullam","H2395":"Hizki","H2268":"Heber","H3461":"Ishmerai","H3152":"Izliah","H3356":"Jakim","H2147":"Zichri","H2067":"Zabdi","H462":"Elienai","H6769":"Zillethai","H447":"Eliel","H5718":"Adaiah","H1256":"Beraiah","H8119":"Shimrath","H8096":"Shimei","H3473":"Ishpan","H5658":"Abdon","H2605":"Hanan","H2608":"Hananiah","H5867":"Elam","H6070":"Anthothijah","H3301":"Iphdeiah","H6439":"Peniel","H8125":"Shamsherai","H7841":"Shehariah","H6271":"Athaliah","H3298":"Jaareshiah","H452":"Elijah","H3395":"Jeroham","H8435":"generation","H3389":"Jerusalem","H1391":"Gibeon","H8034":"name","H4601":"Maacah","H6698":"Zur","H7027":"Kish","H1168":"Baal","H5070":"Nadab","H1446":"Gedor","H2144":"Zecher","H4732":"Mikloth","H8039":"Shimeah","H637":"also","H5048":"before","H251":"male-sibling","H5973":"with","H5369":"Ner","H7586":"Saul","H3083":"Jonathan","H4444":"Malchi-shua","H41":"Abinadab","H792":"Eshbaal","H4807":"Merib-baal","H4318":"Micah","H6377":"Pithon","H4429":"Melech","H8390":"Tarea","H271":"Ahaz","H3085":"Jehoaddah","H5964":"Alemeth","H5820":"Azmaveth","H2174":"Zimri","H4162":"Moza","H1150":"Binea","H501":"Eleasah","H682":"Azel","H8337":"six","H5840":"Azrikam","H1074":"Bocheru","H3458":"Ishmael","H8187":"Sheariah","H5662":"Obadiah","H3605":"all","H6232":"Eshek","H198":"Ulam","H3266":"Jeush","H467":"Eliphelet","H376":"man","H1368":"mighty man","H2428":"Helech","H1869":"to tread","H7198":"bow","H7235":"to multiply","H3967":"hundred","H2572":"fifty"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",33,null,"challoner","Esbaal.... Alias Isboseth."],["challoner",34,null,"challoner","Meribbaal.... Alias Miphiboseth. 2 Kings 4.4."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Benjamin. Some farther particulars of his genealogy are given, for the honour of Saul; (Calmet) and many of those, who returned from captivity, are mentioned, 1 Esdras x. 11. (Sanctius) — Bale, &c., are called Bela, (Menochius; chap. vii. 6.) Jadiel, and Ahiram, or Echi, Numbers xxvi. 36., and Genesis xlvi."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Nohaa is probably styled Bechor; and Rapha, Ros, in Genesis."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Addar, Ared or Hered. (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Ahod, the judge, Judges iii. 15. — Manahath; the Menuchat, in the tribe of Juda, (chap. ii. 54.) after the captivity. (Calmet) — Abulensis thinks Manahath was in Moab, ver. 8. (Menochius)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","And. Literally, “but,” autem. (Haydock) — The sentence is clearer without this particle. (Menochius) — Naaman was the first-born of Ahod; and Gera prevailed on his brethren to leave Gabaa, (Tirinus) and was probably the father of Oza. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Saharim. His father is not specified: but his was a Benjamite. (Menochius) — Some force the Hebrew text to say that Ahod had three sons, whom he sent to form a colony at Manahath, and afterwards he had three others by his two wives, and at last, by Rodes, he had Jobab, &c. (Calmet) — Begot. Protestants, “children in the country of Moab, after he had sent them away: Hushim and Baara were his wives.” But the Septuagint intimate, that “Saharim begot….after he had sent away Osim, and his wife Baara, and he begot of Ada,” &c. (Haydock) — He had perhaps retired into Moab, at the same time as Elimelech, Ruth i. (Tirinus) — Those who were sent away were men, (eos) according to the Hebrew, unless (Haydock) the word should be eas. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Mehusim. Hebrew and Septuagint, “of Husim (ver. 8) he begot.”"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Ono was three miles from Lod, or Diospolis, (Lightfoot) built after the return from Babylon, (1 Esdras ii. 23.; Calmet) in the territory of Benjamin. (Menochius)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","And Sama. They are supposed to have been sons of Elphaal. (Calmet) — Aialon belonged to Dan, Josue xix. 42. But it was near Benjamin, (Menochius) and was probably occupied by people chiefly of that tribe. (Haydock) — Geth. We know not at what period."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Baria, son of Elphaal. Whether the following were his brothers does not appear certain, as it seems more natural to have placed all his father’s children together, ver. 12., and 18."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Semei; perhaps Sama, ver. 13. (Calmet) — Septuagint read, Samai. (Haydock)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Sesac, probably mentioned in ver. 14. (Calmet) — Septuagint have “Sosek,” in both places."],["haydock",28,null,"haydock","Families. Hebrew, “These were heads of the fathers by their generations, chief men.” (Protestants) (Haydock) — Jerusalem, probably after the captivity. We find none of these names from ver. 14, in the days of Josue; and it was not easy for people to establish themselves at Jerusalem, till David had expelled the Jebusites. (Calmet) — Yet we find that the tribes of Juda and Benjamin had seized the lower city long before, Josue xviii. 28. (Haydock)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Dwelt. Literally, “they dwelt.” But Hebrew and Septuagint, “in Gabaon, dwelt the father of Gaboan,” (Haydock) who was called Jehiel, (chap. ix. 35.) or Abiel, 1 Kings ix. 1. (Calmet) — The following verses are repeated in the next chapter, (Haydock) and seem to have been originally the same; though now many variations occur, which shew that “every Hebrew letter” has not been preserved. (Kennicott)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Baal. Add, “and Ner,” (ver. 33., and chap. ix. 36.) as the Septuagint have it here also."],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Zacher, or Zacharias, chap. ix. Hebrew omits the following name, as well as (Haydock) the end of the preceding one. (Kennicott) — The Alexandrian Septuagint has the same faults. But the Vatican retains Macelloth. (Haydock)"],["haydock",32,null,"haydock","Over-against, in the vicinity, while some remained at Gabaon. See Genesis xvi. 12. (Calmet) — The family of Cis resided at Gabaa, 1 Kings x. 26. (Menochius)"],["haydock",33,null,"haydock","Ner, son of Abiel, or of Jeheil, prince of Gabaon, ver. 29. (Haydock) — From Aphia sprung Bechorah, Seror, Abiel, (1 Kings ix.) who is also called Ner and Jehiel. He had Ner, the father of Abner, and Cis, father of Saul. (Menochius) — Abinadab, or Jessui, 1 Kings xiv. 49. (Calmet) — Esbaal, alias Isboseth. (Challoner) — The Hebrews avoid pronouncing the name of the idol, and substitute boseth, “confusion.” (Calmet) — Hence it is clear that many had two names. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",34,null,"haydock","Meribbaal, alias Mephiboseth, 2 Kings iv. 4., (Challoner) and 2 Kings ix. (Menochius)"],["haydock",36,null,"haydock","Joada, or Jara, chap. ix. 42. (Calmet) — “Strange mistakes these!” (Kennicott)"],["haydock",40,null,"haydock","Archers, particularly the natives of Gabaa, Judges xx. 16. Jonathan is praised for his skill, 2 Kings i. 22. (Menochius) — Benjamin. We might hence conclude that here ended the genealogy of that family. Yet we find it partly repeated in the following chapter, ver. 35. (Haydock)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}